00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.710
GANG CHEN: OK,
let's get started.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:35.280
A brief recap what we were
talking the last lecture--

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.660
we emphasized the
density of states,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.840
which is a measure of how
many quantum states either

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.260
per frequency interval,
or per energy interval,

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:51.305
or per view vector,
magnitude interval,

00:00:51.305 --> 00:00:52.640
or wavelength interval.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.680
That's your choice.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.800
And it's really a mathematics.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:03.620
When I want to find the
sum of quantity f over all

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:08.570
the possible [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:13.580
Each setup of this combination
gives me one quantum state

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:16.460
if I include in this
P, polarization,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:21.170
or spin as my addition or,
say, polarization-- generally,

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:23.540
I call this polarization, right?

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:28.940
So when I do this summation, I'm
summing all the quantum states.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:34.610
And when this function
does not vary rapidly

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:38.330
over two adjacent
points of this k,

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:43.520
I can convert this
summation into integration.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:47.480
So the density of
state is really

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:53.630
mathematical convenience,
but it's so widely used--

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:56.580
a very, very powerful tool.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.600
So many times, we
talk about just people

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:00.770
will say, what's the DOS?

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:03.710
I mean, you calculate
the density of states

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:06.110
from different mature systems.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.080
And later on, I hope we
will see some of this

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.020
when we do the, say, computer.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.590
We'll look at what people
are plotting, right?

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:19.070
And then we used
simple relations.

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.870
So depends on this function.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:22.820
Is this function
of function over e?

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.290
A function over k?

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.340
So you decide which one is
the most convenient for you,

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.960
whether you convert this
into e or k integration.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.790
And there is a relation.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.860
So to do that conversion
from k into E,

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:43.880
the relation between the
energy and the wave vector,

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:47.240
for electron, we use a
simple parabolic band.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.680
And [? for ?] phonon/photon is
a linear dispersion that we use.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.220
With that, we did
our math and say,

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.950
what's the corresponding
density of states?

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.790
So that's the emphasis.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.680
We spent the most
amount of time there.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.850
And also, we give example.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:10.580
If you have quantization, right,
the separation of energy level.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.190
Let's say in the z direction,
becomes very large.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:17.390
I cannot convert this summation
into integration anymore.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:22.110
I'll keep that sum, but I still
do-- in the xy right direction,

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.890
I still can convert
that into integration.

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:29.960
So we show the difference in the
density of states of 2D quantum

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:36.590
[? well ?] versus a bulk
paroblic [? biomaterial. ?]

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:39.650
And at the end of
last lecture, we

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:47.400
moved on to talking about
Fermi-Dirac distribution.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.290
This is a now--

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:53.250
I have quantized energy
levels starting from--

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.610
if you recall in
the first chapter,

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.250
we introduced the
Boltzmann Factor,

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:02.850
the probability of a molecule
having certain energy.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.550
And now we expanded that.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:07.050
And we say this is
the Gibbs Factor.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:14.010
This is the probability of a
quantum state being occupied

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:18.149
with energy, E, and with
the number of particle N.

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:20.366
So it's an open system.

00:04:20.366 --> 00:04:23.620
All right, the
constant temperature--

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.570
mu is the chemical potential.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:31.490
That's the energy that it
takes to take out or put

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:35.980
in a particle like that
energy into the system--

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.480
the average energy.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.990
So chemical potential
is a driving force

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:45.530
for mass diffusion, temperature
is the driving force

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:50.060
for heat transfer, and
pressure is the driving force

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:51.260
for the volume change.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:54.420
So those are the
thermodynamic quantities.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.940
And we ask the question.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:00.110
So when we apply
this, we need to find

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:02.660
the normalization
factor A because

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:07.070
of the probability of adding
up all possible possibilities

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:08.480
should give me 1.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.390
So when I apply this to one
quantum state of electron,

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:17.210
a charge-- so it's
[? affirm, ?] [? yeah? ?] Right?

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:20.210
And this quantum state
is in thermal equilibrium

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:21.860
with the surrounding.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:26.690
So in that quantum state, the
electrons then get in, get out.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:28.160
It's an open system.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.190
And there's a constant
temperature communication

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:33.300
with the surrounding electrons.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:39.740
And if I apply the
probability summation,

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.660
I find the normalization factor.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:48.050
And, eventually, I find the
average number of electrons

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:53.400
in that quantum state, E i, is
the Fermi-Dirac distribution--

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.080
E i mass mu over [? KBT ?]
exponential plus one.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:04.100
That's what we
ended last lecture.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.090
And I will continue.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:10.190
So this is for Fermi, right?

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:15.700
Fermi-Dirac-- Fermi--
and in one quantum state,

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:17.710
I can have empty--

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:21.130
no particle there--
no electron there.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:25.480
Or I can have
maximum one, right?

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:27.970
So that summation is easy to do.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:31.060
And now let me extend
that to a boson.

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.940
So with that, I will
say, if it's a boson,

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.860
it's a Bose-Einstein
distribution.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:07:00.190
And again, I consider a
quantum state with the energy--

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:04.540
say, this quantum
state that energy E i.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:05.560
Right?

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:08.080
And boson-- quantums for boson--

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:12.560
one quantum state can have
zero to infinite possibilities,

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:13.060
all right?

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:16.540
It's [INAUDIBLE] the
Pauli exclusion principle.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:26.110
So I can have the number of
particles could be 0, 1, 2--

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.860
so all at this end.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.260
When I have 0--

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.660
the total energy
of the system's 0--

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:34.840
we'll have one that's a E i.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.540
And then we'll have two
particles that's 2 E i.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.750
That's a total energy
of the system, right,

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:47.400
of this specific energy
level, [INAUDIBLE],, OK?

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.910
And, again, then I go
to the Gibbs factor.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:54.630
I say, the probability
of this quantum state

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:57.960
having a particle
between 0 to infinite

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.240
has to sum up equal to 1.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.410
So I sum this up.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:09.330
And that's the small end, right?

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:12.750
[? Factor N. ?] N
to 0 to infinite.

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:18.390
That's [? the ERRA, ?]
exponential mass E. So

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:21.000
that depends on if I
have an N particle.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.390
I should have N times E i.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:27.350
That's the energy.

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:35.659
And then I have N times
mu divided by KBT.

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:36.919
That equals 1.

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:42.460
Right?

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:46.300
So now you take all of this,
and you say E i minus mu.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:48.760
That factor is a common factor.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:52.150
And that is a geometric
[? surface, ?] right?

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:55.690
So if I do my summation for
this geometric [? surface, ?]

00:08:55.690 --> 00:09:01.540
I get 1 minus exponential
minus E i minus mu.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:07.810
KBT equals 1, OK?

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:11.920
Right, so this is the summation
of the geometric [? surface, ?]

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:15.430
and then I have A equals
1 minus exponential

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:18.775
minus E i minus the mu.

00:09:18.775 --> 00:09:19.275
KBT.

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:26.260
So this give me the
normalization factor A.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:31.210
And with this, I have the
probability of this state.

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:36.280
What's the probability of
this state having N particles?

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:37.420
I can write it down.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:41.740
And, again, I can go to find
the average number of particles,

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:42.370
right?

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:44.950
So the average
number of particle--

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:46.570
I use f again.

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:49.120
This is for this energy level.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:53.140
And either say temperature and
[? chemical ?] [? potential ?]

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:55.600
mu for the whole system.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.810
That's the average
number of particles,

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:01.430
and that should equal
to the probability.

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:08.020
So it's N P E,
which we have here--

00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:14.806
E and the N. And this
N equals 0 to infinite.

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:20.490
So I'm finding the average.

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:24.140
So this is when the
average [? award ?]

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:30.520
is N over the probability
I just derived,

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:34.000
was the normalization
factor, right?

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:39.190
So if you sum this up, you're
putting in the N equals 0

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:40.770
to infinite.

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:43.130
And this is the N. This
is my normalization.

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:51.370
It's 1 minus exponential minus
E i minus the mu [? KBT ?]

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:54.350
and times the exponential.

00:10:54.350 --> 00:11:04.330
That's, again, the NEi
minus the N times the mu kBT

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:06.910
And that's the summation.

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:14.240
I should do sum
over all those N.

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.730
Well, there's a small mathematic
trick because N is here,

00:11:19.730 --> 00:11:24.080
N is here, it's not a
simple geometric series.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:33.110
And this mathematic trick is
if you take this function here,

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:37.220
and you do a derivative with
respect to this Ei minus mu,

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:40.130
you get the A in the front.

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:43.610
If you think of this as
my x, that's essentially

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:47.720
is the e minus N times the x.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:50.150
And if you say,
that's my function,

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:53.690
I take the derivative
of that function,

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:56.040
that N will come to the front.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.880
So with that, you can
do this summation.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:00.540
I'm not going into the detail.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:01.700
You can read the book.

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:07.030
And I'm going to give you the
final result of this exercise.

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:16.190
It will give you Ei, and T, mu
the average number of particle

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:22.070
for a boson in one quantum
state in thermal equilibrium

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:24.530
with the surrounding.

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:33.770
This is the Ei minus mu kBT.

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:35.750
Looks very similar.

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:38.210
Before I have [? there ?]
for Fermi-Dirac

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:42.190
I have plus 1, now
I have minus 1.

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:44.890
So Mathematica turns out
a very beautiful-- it's

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:49.070
just the difference between the
two particles is 1 plus, 1 is

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:49.570
minus.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:57.870
And this is the Bose-Einstein
distribution, Bose-Einstein.

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:15.320
And compared to
Fermi-Dirac, if you say here

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:18.080
Ei minus mu divided
by kBT plus 1.

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:26.320
And because here is a plus
1, this numerator is 1,

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:30.070
you say for N here is
always between 0 and 1.

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:37.350
But that's not the case for
Bose-Einstein distribution

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.410
because this is a minus 1.

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:48.580
So if we look at the
typical how it looks like,

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:56.130
And for y let's say the
Fermi-Dirac distribution,

00:13:56.130 --> 00:14:02.040
and because it's an
exponential function, kBT--

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:03.930
remember, kBT at
room temperature

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:08.160
is 26 millielectron-volts.

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:15.370
And if this one, Ei minus
mu, is much larger than kBT,

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:17.272
this was very large.

00:14:17.272 --> 00:14:22.560
because remember I say, if
this is a [? leg ?] here,

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:25.900
that quickly can go to 0.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:29.300
So you apply that,
here is your mu.

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:32.020
And for Fermi-Dirac is 1.

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:36.730
And pretty much all the way,
except the right-hand tool,

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:41.870
the E equals mu range where--

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:47.000
so this is the E of
the energy level.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:51.740
And there's only around
the mu kBT range, 3 kBT.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:55.420
And the rest is either 1 or 0.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:15:01.000
So now if you'll think about
that when we discuss metal

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:05.350
before, we say, OK,
this is your Ef.

00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:08.500
This is your Fermi level.

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:12.890
And Fermi level is actually very
close to the chemical potential

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:14.176
of mu.

00:15:14.176 --> 00:15:15.930
I'll comment that later on.

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:17.600
But just think this is mu.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:27.200
And you say only 3 kBT
near that level you have--

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:30.020
say below this, you
have some empty states.

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:31.790
Normally it's filled.

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:34.440
At zero temperature, it's
filled right to this level.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:37.820
But along zero temperature
because of that Fermi-Dirac

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:40.430
distribution, there are
few empty states below

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:43.735
and a few mobile electron-volts.

00:15:46.810 --> 00:15:49.360
So [? u ?] and metal allow
[? all ?] electrons are moving,

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:51.940
contributing to conduction.

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:54.640
Only near this Ef.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:56.110
So that's one.

00:15:56.110 --> 00:16:01.210
And now if you think about the
Bose-Einstein distribution,

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:10.630
what it look like is here is
your mu and here is your E.

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:14.050
And I cannot have
my E less than mu,

00:16:14.050 --> 00:16:17.760
otherwise the average number
of particles is [? leg ?] here.

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:26.580
So when Ei approach
mu, this approach 1.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:30.600
So that number could
be really large.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:33.460
But Ei is much larger than
mu, this quickly drop.

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:45.060
So this is my
Bose-Einstein distribution.

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:51.370
So you probably already heard
Bose-Einstein condensate.

00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:56.206
A few years ago, the
Physics Department Ketterle

00:16:56.206 --> 00:17:01.030
got a Nobel Prize for the work
in Bose-Einstein condensate.

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:02.420
What is it?

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:09.380
So if you look at this, when
you go to very low temperature,

00:17:09.380 --> 00:17:17.470
this kBT is very small and
10 to the minus 5 Kelvin.

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:22.119
And then when this one gets
to, say, really close to mu,

00:17:22.119 --> 00:17:30.430
it turns out this number is
getting really very large.

00:17:30.430 --> 00:17:32.390
This goes very large.

00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:37.960
So what they means is
this one energy state

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:42.890
that all the particles fall into
that energy state, one quantum

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:44.390
state.

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:47.930
So they condense into
that quantum state.

00:17:47.930 --> 00:17:51.680
That's the simple
Bose-Einstein condensate.

00:17:51.680 --> 00:17:54.230
And the trick is you
got to get that, say,

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:56.600
temperature really, really low.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:00.680
So it turns out that that
does say there was a--

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:04.040
Einstein, this was predicted
a very, very long time ago,

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:06.410
but nobody were able to
drive to that temperature.

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:10.640
And the Physics Department
and Ketterle and a few others

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:14.060
have been trying to drive
this to a very low temperature

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:19.820
for many years by using various
