00:57:39.250 --> 00:57:43.210
But in theory you still have
to actually determine that mu.

00:57:46.200 --> 00:57:50.520
How you determine that
mu is for electron,

00:57:50.520 --> 00:57:55.230
you have fixed
number of electrons.

00:57:55.230 --> 00:57:59.670
So the total number of electron
you know in the system.

00:57:59.670 --> 00:58:01.350
And that total
number of electrons,

00:58:01.350 --> 00:58:10.820
again, I do this summation, is--

00:58:10.820 --> 00:58:16.375
so each state, the average
number of electrons

00:58:16.375 --> 00:58:19.510
is Fermi-Dirac.

00:58:19.510 --> 00:58:28.090
So I have E minus mu kBT plus 1.

00:58:28.090 --> 00:58:32.445
So I have one equation
here, another equation here.

00:58:35.300 --> 00:58:38.510
And using this
equation I can say

00:58:38.510 --> 00:58:43.910
if you gave me N I can find mu.

00:58:43.910 --> 00:58:46.460
I substitute mu back
into here, which

00:58:46.460 --> 00:58:50.210
is a function of temperature,
mu is a function of temperature,

00:58:50.210 --> 00:58:53.150
I substitute it back
here, I take a derivative,

00:58:53.150 --> 00:58:57.830
now I have one temperature
here, another temperature there.

00:58:57.830 --> 00:59:01.700
So it's more complicated.

00:59:01.700 --> 00:59:02.630
But it's doable.

00:59:02.630 --> 00:59:06.850
Mathematically, [? I'm ?]
[? closed ?] problem.

