01:08:06.090 --> 01:08:12.020
I can find the all quantum
state of this system.

01:08:12.020 --> 01:08:16.760
The total number,
total quantum state--

01:08:16.760 --> 01:08:21.500
quantum states-- let's
say total number is omega.

01:08:24.580 --> 01:08:32.380
So when I construct an ensemble,
I look at each quantum state

01:08:32.380 --> 01:08:37.330
is one system, each
of this as one system.

01:08:41.519 --> 01:08:43.784
You can see that's
a huge, huge number.

01:08:47.290 --> 01:08:49.340
And then in each of
this quantum state

01:08:49.340 --> 01:08:52.540
this quantity has
a certain value.

01:08:52.540 --> 01:08:58.330
I may have a quantum
state where, say, all--

01:08:58.330 --> 01:09:00.189
there is no particle there.

01:09:03.140 --> 01:09:06.770
In that case, the energy
of that whole system

01:09:06.770 --> 01:09:08.210
in that quantum state is zero.

01:09:11.760 --> 01:09:17.069
So each of this I have,
let's say, this quantity.

01:09:17.069 --> 01:09:23.160
And then what I do is
the ensemble average

01:09:23.160 --> 01:09:25.840
is, what's the probability--

01:09:25.840 --> 01:09:29.700
so now I'm saying the
probability of observing

01:09:29.700 --> 01:09:33.439
this quantum state is p.

01:09:33.439 --> 01:09:36.060
So what I do is xiPi.

01:09:38.689 --> 01:09:45.445
And the i's sum up all quantum
states in this ensemble.

01:09:49.779 --> 01:09:52.630
So here I have the time average.

01:09:52.630 --> 01:09:55.420
Here is the ensemble average.

01:09:55.420 --> 01:09:58.530
So in statistical
treatment is mostly

01:09:58.530 --> 01:10:00.975
